Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["x", "TI"],
 " is a particle, a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["CyclicWord"]], "paclet:ChernSimons/ref/CyclicWord", 
    "ChernSimons Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "4ef4db0d-a27e-4a8a-8ece-a16b8d721a56"],
 " or the list of its particles, a product of cyclic words, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["HBar"]], "paclet:ChernSimons/ref/HBar", 
    "ChernSimons Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "599fce4f-b20c-4ef1-859b-9bebeb4460d1"],
 ", or a sum of these of one degree."
}], "Notes",
 CellFrameLabels->{{Inherited, Inherited}, {Inherited, 
    Cell[
     TextData[{
      "26.09.30: Design review - one degree function for everything the \
paclet builds, which replaced CyclicWordDegree and PlanckDegree in R5f, on \
2026-09-30, with no aliases. The grading is always given and has no default: \
the old two-argument CyclicWordDegree meant the bar degree, and with the \
grading explicit each use says which of the three degrees it reads, as the \
rule of explicit names asks; a default of \"Bar\" would have been one more \
rule. HBar has a degree only in the symmetric grading, 2(n - 3), the degree \
that makes the operator of the Beilinson-Drinfeld algebra homogeneous of \
degree -1; in the exterior grading q110 and q120 already differ in degree, so \
no degree of HBar would mean anything there, and HBar returns unevaluated in \
the other two gradings. A particle has the degree of the word made of it \
alone, as the paper writes [x] = -2 on the circle, and a scalar multiple of a \
particle counts only when the scalar contains no particle, since particles \
may be monomials such as y z. An Association of degrees is accepted in the \
bar grading only, which the normalization of CyclicWord and \
GenerateCyclicWords relies on. Prior art: the Wolfram Language has no degree \
function on graded words; Exponent and VertexDegree measure other degrees. \
T12/paclet-element-degree pins the three gradings against the degBar, degC \
and degC1 of the engine the verification suites load, on every word to length \
5 in both conventions.", "\n\n", 
       Cell[
        BoxData[
         GridBox[{{
            Cell[
             BoxData[
              ButtonBox[
               DynamicBox[
                FEPrivate`FrontEndResource[
                "DocumentationToolsStrings", "Edit"]], BaseStyle -> 
               "TextAnnotationButton"]]], 
            StyleBox[
             RowBox[{
               DynamicBox[
                FEPrivate`FrontEndResource[
                "DocumentationToolsStrings", "LastAnnotator"]], "pavel"}], 
             "TextAnnotator"], 
            Cell[
             BoxData[
              ButtonBox[
               DynamicBox[
                FEPrivate`FrontEndResource[
                "DocumentationToolsStrings", "Delete"]], BaseStyle -> 
               "TextAnnotationRemoveButton"]]], ""}}, 
          GridBoxAlignment -> {
           "Columns" -> {Left, Left, Right, Right}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}, 
          GridBoxItemSize -> {"Columns" -> {
              Scaled[0.15], 
              Scaled[0.68], 
              Scaled[0.15], 
              Scaled[0.02]}}]]], "\n"}], "TextAnnotation", 
     CellSize -> {590, Inherited}]}},
 CellTags->"TextAnnotation",
 CellID->418505534,ExpressionUUID->"e39355c1-2b0c-4ede-bd47-540bab49df41"],

